## What's Real vs. Simulated

Being upfront about this matters for evaluating the project honestly:

| Component | Status |
|---|---|
| Broker connections & order placement | **Real** — actual Alpaca/IBKR paper trading accounts |
| Trade history & user accounts | **Real** — persisted in SQLite |
| Historical backtest | **Real** — actual market data via yfinance |
| AI Assistant | **Real** — live LLM calls via Groq |
| Live dashboard price chart | **Simulated** — random-walk demo data, not a live market feed |
| Price Forecast page | **Simulated math** — EMA/linear regression/Monte Carlo projections, not a trained ML model |
| Automated bot's live trading loop | **Not connected to a live feed** — the strategy logic is implemented and backtested successfully, but the dashboard's automated signal currently reacts to simulated ticks rather than the real Alpaca/IBKR price stream |

---

## Known Limitations

- No real-time market data feed powering the *live* automated bot loop (backtesting uses real data; the live dashboard chart does not, yet)
- No role-based access control — every logged-in user currently has the same permissions
- Session tokens are stored in memory and don't survive a server restart (trade history and user accounts do, since those are in the database)
- The AI Assistant has general context about the bot's strategy but doesn't yet see live account state (balance, open positions) when answering
- No automated test suite
- Not deployed to a live server — runs locally only

